    kicker: "New Mexico \u00b7 Adult-Use Market",
    intro: "For operators building in New Mexico, the engineering brief is shaped by two facts: New Mexico legalized adult-use cannabis in 2021 with a deliberately microbusiness-friendly framework: low fees, no plant-count caps for micros, and vertically integrated options. Low barriers created a cultivation surge - over a thousand licensed producers - followed by price compression. Efficient, low-cost-per-gram facility design is the survival strategy.",
    perspective: "My approach in New Mexico is license-first, climate-second. The Cannabis Control Division licenses producers (including microbusinesses), manufacturers, and retailers with relatively low fees. Local zoning applies. Once the compliance path is clear, the defining engineering work is environmental: High desert: intense sun, low humidity, cool nights. Excellent for greenhouse light-deprivation; indoor facilities need cooling sized for high solar gain with easy VPD control. I size dehumidification from transpiration schedules and cooling from lighting layouts, then document everything for plan review.",
    topics: ["Facility security, camera coverage, and vault design per the Cannabis Control Division rules", "High desert: intense sun, low humidity, cool nights.", "License-application engineering support and inspection readiness", "Drying, curing, and post-harvest room design", "Electrical service upgrades and utility coordination", "Structural review for rooftop units, racking, and multi-tier loads", "Fertigation, RO water, and drainage design", "Commissioning and environmental controls verification"],
