    intro: "Cannabis facility engineering in New York starts with OCM: New York legalized adult-use cannabis under the MRTA in 2021. The Office of Cannabis Management's rollout stumbled early (CAURD program, lawsuits) but retail licensing accelerated from 2024, with cultivation scaling across the state. The sleeping giant of East Coast cannabis: massive demand, maturing retail, and cultivation scaling from legacy Hudson Valley and Long Island growers to large indoor facilities. Two-tier market (NYC metro vs. upstate) needs different facility strategies.",
    perspective: "My approach in New York is license-first, climate-second. OCM licenses cultivators by tier (including microbusiness), with conditional cultivator licenses converting to full licenses. True-and-actual-party-interest rules are strict. Once the compliance path is clear, the defining engineering work is environmental: Varied: NYC metro is humid subtropical (dehumidification-driven), upstate is cold continental (heating-dominated). Long Island greenhouse belt has its own microclimate. I size dehumidification from transpiration schedules and cooling from lighting layouts, then document everything for plan review.",
