    faqs: [
      { question: "Who regulates cannabis cultivation in Ohio?", answer: "Ohio Division of Cannabis Control (Department of Commerce) oversees cannabis licensing in Ohio. Ohio voters approved adult-use cannabis (Issue 2) in 2023, with sales beginning August 2024. The Division of Cannabis Control regulates both programs while the legislature reworked the statute (SB 56)." },
      { question: "How does Ohio licensing work for cultivation facilities?", answer: "The Division of Cannabis Control issues cultivator licenses in levels by canopy size; medical operators received priority for adult-use conversion. Apex Grid sequences engineering deliverables to the licensing milestones so drawings are ready when the application or inspection requires them." },
      { question: "How are drying and curing rooms designed?", answer: "Around 60F and 60 percent relative humidity with gentle, uniform airflow and no light - the '60/60' slow dry over 10-14 days. Drying rooms need dedicated dehumidification sized to the wet harvest load, which arrives all at once, unlike the steady transpiration of a flower room. With Ohio's Great Lakes continental, this is the system that separates a stable crop from a failed one." },
      { question: "How do PPFD targets translate to electrical and cooling loads?", answer: "Flowering cannabis typically targets 600-900 micromoles per square meter per second of photosynthetic photon flux density. Delivering that takes roughly 35-50 watts per square foot with modern LEDs (more with HPS), and nearly all of it becomes heat: about 3.41 BTU per hour per watt. A lighting layout therefore sets the electrical service size, panel schedule, and cooling tonnage in one move. the Ohio Division of Cannabis Control expects to see this engineering documented when Ohio facilities go through inspection." },
      { question: "How is grow-room dehumidification sized?", answer: "From transpiration, not from rules of thumb. Cannabis transpires roughly 97 percent of irrigation water; a flowering canopy can release 0.1-0.15 gallons per square foot per day. Size dehumidification in pints per day at AHRI conditions against the irrigation schedule, then verify the HVAC can remove that moisture without overcooling the room - which usually means dedicated dehumidifiers plus reheat, not just a bigger air conditioner. In Ohio, Great Lakes continental sets the design week this system must survive." },
    ],
