    faqs: [
      { question: "Where do cultivation facilities locate in Aberdeen?", answer: "Aberdeen anchors the Brown County cultivation market. Northeast ag region with grain-handling infrastructure." },
      { question: "What approvals does a Aberdeen grow facility need?", answer: "Aberdeen zoning and building permits plus the South Dakota Department of Health, Medical Cannabis Program state licensing. Apex Grid coordinates the local permit set with the state facility requirements so neither approval blocks the other." },
      { question: "How does biosecurity shape facility design?", answer: "Through compartmentalization: separate HVAC zones per flower room so pathogens cannot travel on shared air, airlocks and pressure cascades between clean and dirty zones, washable surfaces, and protocols for people and material movement. Hop latent viroid and powdery mildew have destroyed harvests that shared air between rooms. the South Dakota Department of Health, Medical Cannabis Program expects to see this engineering documented when Aberdeen facilities go through inspection." },
      { question: "How do PPFD targets translate to electrical and cooling loads?", answer: "Flowering cannabis typically targets 600-900 micromoles per square meter per second of photosynthetic photon flux density. Delivering that takes roughly 35-50 watts per square foot with modern LEDs (more with HPS), and nearly all of it becomes heat: about 3.41 BTU per hour per watt. A lighting layout therefore sets the electrical service size, panel schedule, and cooling tonnage in one move. With Aberdeen's northern plains cold, this is the system that separates a stable crop from a failed one." },
      { question: "What is VPD and why does it drive HVAC design?", answer: "Vapor pressure deficit measures the drying power of the air around the leaf - the difference between saturated and actual vapor pressure. Cannabis transpires best at roughly 0.8-1.2 kPa in veg and 1.0-1.5 kPa in flower. Holding VPD means holding temperature AND humidity simultaneously in a tight band, which is why grow HVAC uses dedicated dehumidification with reheat rather than comfort cooling alone. With Aberdeen's northern plains cold, this is the system that separates a stable crop from a failed one." },
    ],
